WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:11.659
"Collagen Supplements for Arthritis"

00:00:11.836 --> 00:00:13.680
Nearly a millennium ago,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.520
a medieval nun suggested eating
gelatin to reduce joint pain.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.180
Unfortunately, when it was put
to the test in a multicenter,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.220
randomized double-blind
placebo-controlled trial,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.840
it didn't work.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.500
A dozen years later, looking
at knee and hip osteoarthritis,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.480
there was still
no overall evidence

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.820
for collagen supplements
working better than placebo.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.480
However, the latest compilation
of studies purported to show

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.880
a benefit for improving
osteoarthritis symptoms.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.700
In one symptom index, it
appeared to help with stiffness,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.260
but not pain or disability.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.960
But, an overall
improvement in symptoms

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.560
and a different index of pain
were statistically significant.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.520
If, however, you exclude
the smallest study,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.740
whose authors were employees
of a collagen company,

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.440
all of the results
lost clinical importance,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.160
meaning the overall effects
were unnoticeably small.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.540
In fact, every single one was
funded by collagen companies,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.940
or directly run
by company employees.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.180
Another thing you'll
notice about these data

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.940
is that with this one exception,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.640
the smaller the study,
the larger the effect.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.900
This pattern is a common sign
of publication bias,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.420
the tendency to do
a whole bunch of small trials,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.120
but publish only the ones
that work to your advantage

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.800
and secretly shelve the rest.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.200
Coupled with all of
the conflicts of interest,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.580
concerns have been raised

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.680
that additional small studies
may have been conducted

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.660
but conveniently disappeared.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.020
And then, other fishy findings
are that smaller doses

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.460
appeared to have larger effects,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.100
as did using it
for a shorter amount of time.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.760
Okay, but the last study
was published in 2016.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.380
What's happened since then?

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.040
A comprehensive systematic
review published in 2022

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.140
was surprised to note
that the last study done

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.060
on collagen supplements
for rheumatoid arthritis

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.740
was published
more than a decade ago,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.900
and the last osteoarthritis
study was still that 2016 one.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.860
Why?

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.960
Well, one possible reason

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.560
is the high incidence of
adverse side effects reported

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.060
in those taking
collagen supplements,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.920
or another reason may be

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.020
that they simply
don't work very well.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.440
I mean, if collagen company
executives truly believed

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.060
they were selling
something that worked,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.280
why don't they do more studies?

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.680
If they thought collagen had
a decent chance of working,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.260
they could make out
like bandits.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.020
The fact that they haven't
sponsored such a trial

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.880
suggests they're
not so confident

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.260
it would actually show benefit.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.300
One study has been published
since 2016, the biggest yet,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.220
with more than 150 people.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.320
Funded by a collagen company,

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.840
researchers found
a significant drop in knee pain,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.480
and a significant
improvement in knee function

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.060
among those randomized
to collagen supplements.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.480
But they also found a
significant drop in knee pain

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.830
and improvement in knee function

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.440
in those randomized
to the placebo,

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.860
with no real difference
between them.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.680
So, the fact that
effectively a sugar pill

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.080
worked as well as
the collagen supplements

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.180
suggests that
collagen doesn't work.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.540
But that does raise
another point.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.400
If you look at what
was used for controls

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.360
in the collagen studies,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.820
it was basically
just sugar or sawdust.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.640
And the same with the
skin studies I talked about

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.420
in the last video.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.020
So, even if collagen was found
to be helpful in some way,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.660
there is no proof
that collagen has any effects,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.180
beyond just being
a source of amino acids

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.080
like any other protein.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.780
So, if any purported
collagen effect

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.300
is just a matter of increased
amino acid availability,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.400
then why not just, like,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.620
have some extra hummus
or something?

